## Key Takeaways

| Concept | Detail |
|---|---|
| Anti-debug technique | `ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME)` — returns -1 under a debugger |
| Bypass method | Intercept syscall with `catch syscall ptrace`, force `$rax = 0` |
| Password leak | Binary printed password in plaintext before `strncmp` |
| Flag | `HTB{0ld3st_tr1ck_1n_th3_b00k}` |

### Why `ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME)`?

When a process calls `ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME)`, it signals to the OS that it wants to be traced by its parent. If a debugger is already attached, the call fails with `EPERM`. Malware and CTF binaries abuse this as a cheap anti-debug: if `ptrace` fails → debugger detected → bail out.

The fix is always the same: catch the syscall and zero out the return value before the binary can check it.

